{
  "schema": "research-public-artifact/v1",
  "concept": "Field-name abbreviation trade-offs",
  "slug": "field-name-abbreviation-trade-offs",
  "canonical_concept_id": "concept:fd7891991ea78245588d8b9d0d35ddf4055ae76689091e1a19af1fd0be4d34c8",
  "canonical_artifact_sha256": "c2450e9abdfa21e55b1f6f629717ab7678934327111df7644ad30ae35ac09f87",
  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "clm:1d8aa372665722df4eb111076f0b98f3d33e86bc5994cfa94dd161a791001934",
      "text": "BSON encodes each element with a type marker and a null-terminated field name. With values and structure unchanged, shortening a field name by k UTF-8 bytes saves k bytes per occurrence in ordinary uncompressed BSON; repeated occurrences in embedded documents multiply that saving. This byte-count rule is derived from the specification. (high confidence)",
      "source_url": "https://bsonspec.org/spec.html",
      "also": [
        "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/v7.0/data-modeling/best-practices/",
        "https://richardstartin.github.io/posts/shrinking-bson-documents"
      ],
      "validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "ev:bfb04d2ddefa3e8dc2ae10a887d3080cd567916b1bba1d7939fa50b26d55b29d"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:a8738bcd759a6c7a65b8aa4a7fd340e025ebe971cb36dec280097aded884c1c7",
      "text": "MongoDB 7.0 data-modeling guidance says shortening field names does not reduce index size because those indexes have a predefined structure without field names. This guidance should not be generalized here to every specialized index representation. (medium confidence)",
      "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/v7.0/data-modeling/best-practices/",
      "also": [
        "https://www.percona.com/blog/2016/03/24/percona-how-to-field-names-and-document-size-in-mongodb/"
      ],
      "validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "ev:4685547dc8b618cdcab2cb1f5898f23f31479fcefb9384b96470953a5c024a8c",
        "ev:e347628a2c421ec855fb5b0bd63e99f8183118cfbfab397e055cd22110fd3b2e"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:28851044eb8871b3a6c7dbb4ff1409a3101584fc97bc68da796060cece5f9f58",
      "text": "MongoDB stores all field names in every document, so shortening them matters most for small documents, where field names may represent a proportionally large amount of space. Sources: https://www.mongodb.com/docs/v7.0/data-modeling/best-practices/",
      "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/v7.0/data-modeling/best-practices/",
      "also": [
        "https://www.percona.com/blog/percona-how-to-field-names-and-document-size-in-mongodb/"
      ],
      "validation_status": "REMEDIATED-PROJECTION",
      "origin_validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [],
      "origin_evidence_ids": [
        "ev:456bd1d1e38f280ddc42f53ec94f743c7acf10bb81096fed121d2f18a6e670c6",
        "ev:85c3601f01a149592bf76c09b26fcdffaa3fcb2fea39369eb43861db3c94e541"
      ],
      "remediation": {
        "schema": "claim-remediation-projection/v1",
        "record": "remediation.json",
        "requires_revalidation": true,
        "claim_text_sha256": "3794171e02330aa7c7b1077384d276ef06aeede20366eb64e78a9d543df171e4"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:138fa287c35da870729acadcda7db84a9e5edbe58d0ed181cf7104252e92a219",
      "text": "Disk compression does not eliminate name bytes from uncompressed BSON in the database cache. Name shortening can therefore improve cache density even when compressed disk savings are modest; a latency improvement still requires workload measurement. (medium confidence)",
      "source_url": "https://richardstartin.github.io/posts/shrinking-bson-documents",
      "also": [
        "https://smalldatum.blogspot.com/2014/12/storage-overhead-for-attribute-names-in.html",
        "https://www.mongodb.com/blog/post/new-compression-options-mongodb-30"
      ],
      "validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "ev:5a998b4cb656f71b1cc4e8a0b004bfa6055249c9666cf00c90aa4b0d9362cd02",
        "ev:c879220b4901f65ac4b4d07a45395b59aa97ed4b63c3bba178632e09c74ef695",
        "ev:e8427ea11c4d7969ebdc7c2e1812560843104b13720c326bd62476917cf6c213"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:fadd6c7f8ab99223b4d868bfc2cef9d9914dbbd4678fc4131f54c225d0ddf8af",
      "text": "Field-name abbreviation has its largest proportional effect for small documents, where field names may represent a proportionally large amount of space. MongoDB's documentation illustrates a nine-byte saving per document by replacing last_name with lname and best_score with score. Sources: https://www.mongodb.com/docs/v7.0/data-modeling/best-practices/",
      "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/v7.0/data-modeling/best-practices/",
      "also": [
        "https://bsonspec.org/spec.html"
      ],
      "validation_status": "REMEDIATED-PROJECTION",
      "origin_validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [],
      "origin_evidence_ids": [
        "ev:85c3601f01a149592bf76c09b26fcdffaa3fcb2fea39369eb43861db3c94e541",
        "ev:bfb04d2ddefa3e8dc2ae10a887d3080cd567916b1bba1d7939fa50b26d55b29d"
      ],
      "remediation": {
        "schema": "claim-remediation-projection/v1",
        "record": "remediation.json",
        "requires_revalidation": true,
        "claim_text_sha256": "e36693ce52b4a85b00c65095ed738b552dd8ed8443ec827784e494dbed06e441"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:7000fc36128914b6271a2862668a16b0d33704a06b839f4db85b623663339f2d",
      "text": "Prefer concise names that preserve meaning. Very short field names make documents much harder for people to read and understand. An application mapping can keep descriptive names in code while shorter names are stored. Sources: https://www.mongodb.com/company/blog/technical/difference-field-name-makes-reduce-document-size-increase-performance ; https://www.percona.com/blog/percona-how-to-field-names-and-document-size-in-mongodb/",
      "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/company/blog/technical/difference-field-name-makes-reduce-document-size-increase-performance",
      "also": [
        "https://richardstartin.github.io/posts/shrinking-bson-documents",
        "https://www.percona.com/blog/2016/03/24/percona-how-to-field-names-and-document-size-in-mongodb/",
        "https://www.percona.com/blog/percona-how-to-field-names-and-document-size-in-mongodb/"
      ],
      "validation_status": "REMEDIATED-PROJECTION",
      "origin_validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [],
      "origin_evidence_ids": [
        "ev:0cf60fb1a81d3d8d6479826798ce3a2e53f1277e2ea9017db77b49b57d362e46",
        "ev:35528f2d2240ac33f0bc899ccc4060f520f9412e3124a47149bedf38b8382beb",
        "ev:63bdd8a938634542e311d553d97c5d21ec0f8d1df4f5383c84d63209fa364078"
      ],
      "remediation": {
        "schema": "claim-remediation-projection/v1",
        "record": "remediation.json",
        "requires_revalidation": true,
        "claim_text_sha256": "8bc3ef4ebc0051dce7f8746709ee24b5505180137ba8a23d2f5ac27196d97039"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:740deb8a97db6508b9d214e6e26dc77a856bcdbc6717b13d218630cd80ef102a",
      "text": "Percona's March 24, 2016 example reports BSON sizes of 54,879 and 41,392 bytes after abbreviating names, approximately a 24.6% reduction. These are Object.bsonsize measurements on one constructed document, not compressed disk measurements or a general savings percentage. (low confidence)",
      "source_url": "https://www.percona.com/blog/2016/03/24/percona-how-to-field-names-and-document-size-in-mongodb/",
      "also": [
        "https://www.percona.com/blog/percona-how-to-field-names-and-document-size-in-mongodb/"
      ],
      "validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "ev:a357fb689c5a9741a13ccc24737ac9f68028be76c403047464be4ec36525c715",
        "ev:b2c98b4596771061326aa06011b2489d9fdb8d874f9a7d5a5b23d2e48da42de5",
        "ev:cb5f009c9e446b31a8a8879cb6509ba2a87ce8245b3c739d63a57f69c1710f70"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:4f40c38457af2de74fbb12628f5af102d08aa00b63b2dc601ed35df512827f7e",
      "text": "Allegro's January 14, 2021 MongoDB 4.4 experiment used one-million-document collections with compression enabled and measured storageSize minus freeStorageSize. Flattening and shortening user fields reduced the reported non-key portion from 3.4 MB to 2 MB. That comparison combines structural and naming changes and excludes the key portion; it does not establish a 41% reduction in the whole collection or isolate abbreviation alone. (low confidence)",
      "source_url": "https://blog.allegro.tech/2021/01/impact-of-the-data-model-on-the-MongoDB-database-size.html",
      "also": [],
      "validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "ev:305330f720c99cf0569ba36add0044b7ae1d92cb976ff998cb043a170bb24c1c",
        "ev:6fa1fbeb885b4a1c2c3092ae227274ea800050b48bb75d9cbe4a37b8563d74ac",
        "ev:d6d8cb0b4b5b3ffce4da1c5b0366d0eae2a2118aa9c4ac078577630941a8e5b2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:b19cc35facc8a58cf11092607f337855640124d775d1117587ca0c21d80b4625",
      "text": "Raw BSON savings cannot be equated with compressed disk savings. MongoDB's compression article says field names compress well in documents and that the additional benefits of short field names are modest; Percona's analysis likewise notes that compression covers only some of the places where field-name size matters (on disk and in the journal), while cache memory, network and application memory are also affected. Sources: https://www.mongodb.com/blog/post/new-compression-options-mongodb-30 ; https://www.percona.com/blog/percona-how-to-field-names-and-document-size-in-mongodb/",
      "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/blog/post/new-compression-options-mongodb-30",
      "also": [
        "https://richardstartin.github.io/posts/shrinking-bson-documents",
        "https://smalldatum.blogspot.com/2014/12/storage-overhead-for-attribute-names-in.html",
        "https://www.percona.com/blog/percona-how-to-field-names-and-document-size-in-mongodb/"
      ],
      "validation_status": "REMEDIATED-PROJECTION",
      "origin_validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [],
      "origin_evidence_ids": [
        "ev:07a922e41baaa7f285f3180916a53c32632ca564996b7be4c8934f9a0ae7bd03",
        "ev:6087a79d1e08800625eec83cbe6ec3d59c59011abe2d8e0a6ea1ae042813291b",
        "ev:dc0e0102deeba18c3008d4da803b4e6b192fb505370976dd0525c3330d7e21f8"
      ],
      "remediation": {
        "schema": "claim-remediation-projection/v1",
        "record": "remediation.json",
        "requires_revalidation": true,
        "claim_text_sha256": "b71618abee78c17d7e26a91d616b216362cdca6db33f7896a2af76443071ce3c"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:1f6082081e5a8f2430fbbf1f3f89d61cae9e88128fa2f3d49ecc826f298cb289",
      "text": "Evaluate abbreviation by loading your data with and without compression enabled and comparing the sizes (for example storageSize and indexSize from db.stats()), and weigh readability alongside savings: MongoDB cautions that single-letter field names or codes can obscure the data and make it harder to use. The benefit of short field names under compression is modest. Sources: https://www.mongodb.com/blog/post/new-compression-options-mongodb-30",
      "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/blog/post/new-compression-options-mongodb-30",
      "also": [
        "https://blog.allegro.tech/2021/01/impact-of-the-data-model-on-the-MongoDB-database-size.html",
        "https://richardstartin.github.io/posts/shrinking-bson-documents"
      ],
      "validation_status": "REMEDIATED-PROJECTION",
      "origin_validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [],
      "origin_evidence_ids": [
        "ev:0866ab96b5348bfb9254a6f157c1cd76a7dcb2bf177af25174edf37711a7eba7",
        "ev:0cf60fb1a81d3d8d6479826798ce3a2e53f1277e2ea9017db77b49b57d362e46",
        "ev:55682d6ab0ac8296fc77594c24fcea95765e3ba82b4178b08d8d91b2f947d652",
        "ev:c879220b4901f65ac4b4d07a45395b59aa97ed4b63c3bba178632e09c74ef695",
        "ev:eea9ffbdc75b9c6bccc2fedf222e53dc5dcdcbe615e35153c702b2197023cb9b"
      ],
      "remediation": {
        "schema": "claim-remediation-projection/v1",
        "record": "remediation.json",
        "requires_revalidation": true,
        "claim_text_sha256": "efb097d158eb63f3cd5aac5f658bcd8d9b9db78e6e657a051e119819855d4888"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:948cffa86a2e643de3f0d346f864b8cc620ba147fb16525a3bff105eaa756caa",
      "text": "In Small Datum's December 26, 2014 two-billion-document experiment, the long-name/short-name database-size ratio was about 1.01 with WiredTiger zlib, compared with 1.53 for mmapv1. The deliberately tiny values and historical engines limit generalization. (low confidence)",
      "source_url": "https://smalldatum.blogspot.com/2014/12/storage-overhead-for-attribute-names-in.html",
      "also": [],
      "validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "ev:1966e0633a0281bd81f7598dd65f1a7a49155c40bcb31b8d44c35432ecd11a4b"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:ad7f5e9b3169f8983ea2378ba8b9e577b077c8363b0d115597edca40bc5f5470",
      "text": "Removing JSON whitespace is different from shortening names: BSON stores structure, names, and typed values rather than JSON formatting whitespace. Abbreviating a name changes encoded bytes; pretty-print removal alone does not. (high confidence)",
      "source_url": "https://bsonspec.org/spec.html",
      "also": [
        "https://groups.google.com/g/mongodb-user/c/sG2SH3ecyYg"
      ],
      "validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "ev:74cf08e23793bfc0347fa3da4c9bb7f1ee0a002db07eb22ed087e9f43f8c1534"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:f153e4f5d691dac61ce2741e1c913552c37aac3084b8cbd22e1fcc1665682810",
      "text": "Cryptic single-letter names reduce expressiveness. An application mapping, kept in a mapping collection or in application code, can retain descriptive application names while storing abbreviations. Sources: https://www.percona.com/blog/percona-how-to-field-names-and-document-size-in-mongodb/",
      "source_url": "https://www.percona.com/blog/percona-how-to-field-names-and-document-size-in-mongodb/",
      "also": [
        "https://www.mongodb.com/blog/post/new-compression-options-mongodb-30"
      ],
      "validation_status": "REMEDIATED-PROJECTION",
      "origin_validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [],
      "origin_evidence_ids": [
        "ev:8f2fdc63a270088625e4d3e515bb09e7df2199fab32687699fb758e89c8336d7",
        "ev:f03a9d871b91df003c4b53a2a6632b4d14314969b87aff9a7bd9b7f4b9986d2a"
      ],
      "remediation": {
        "schema": "claim-remediation-projection/v1",
        "record": "remediation.json",
        "requires_revalidation": true,
        "claim_text_sha256": "27c9dfe13a183d56d6338ed4e45a908fc988e8dd0aa7839efd9c6eefe528840f"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:9af21ee48ed0cee68743d34b00ec8410270c748085755caebf9365b51984e54d",
      "text": "Raw BSON savings alone do not establish infrastructure savings: field-name size affects several places (application memory, network, replication log, cache, compressor input, disk, journal), and compression covers only some of them, so storage and cache or workload behavior should be assessed separately. Sources: https://www.percona.com/blog/percona-how-to-field-names-and-document-size-in-mongodb/",
      "source_url": "https://www.percona.com/blog/percona-how-to-field-names-and-document-size-in-mongodb/",
      "also": [
        "https://richardstartin.github.io/posts/shrinking-bson-documents",
        "https://smalldatum.blogspot.com/2014/12/storage-overhead-for-attribute-names-in.html"
      ],
      "validation_status": "REMEDIATED-PROJECTION",
      "origin_validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [],
      "origin_evidence_ids": [
        "ev:1d1bd72343cbf295edd0f5af0f40667341735c62ea1352e8d672c49b4730b329",
        "ev:2d5ab87d2db055c2e8f6967cc98c1b47a750c61e7de7341f39b805803ac18e8e",
        "ev:72d6a6a7019f6c1479fdd3d7cf7d79dddb292f6a9f9f7d56587b32a308b2e7dd"
      ],
      "remediation": {
        "schema": "claim-remediation-projection/v1",
        "record": "remediation.json",
        "requires_revalidation": true,
        "claim_text_sha256": "52178273f6fbf47f05f753180b700db40f190ce4a925a785a8cf9f1a9c80fa09"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "clm:cd4a38a16411f881396ef9799e04e680a28ee4f4caa12bc01a4383193919db6c",
      "text": "Shorter BSON documents can let more documents fit in cache and reduce uncompressed application payload size. The sources do not establish a universal latency improvement or a fixed storage-bill reduction from abbreviating names. (medium confidence)",
      "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/company/blog/technical/difference-field-name-makes-reduce-document-size-increase-performance",
      "also": [
        "https://www.percona.com/blog/2016/03/24/percona-how-to-field-names-and-document-size-in-mongodb/"
      ],
      "validation_status": "SUPPORTED",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "ev:67f2fd5c6514448f2e3ca2fe34bf3e3d6d4556f7f895e94081558aa35e9dae36",
        "ev:faa4f94c618f768e26b6724fb8075361f82f47e344641794e5983a7bfa03edf4"
      ]
    }
  ],
